Still missing:
- Extend convergent only nozzle to separated fluxes

Fuel:
    fuelProperties { calorific 43e6; }   lower heating value in J/kg
  Optional: if the block is missing the heating value is 42e6 J/kg. The same value is
  used by the main combustor and the afterburner, and by ProJet Off-Design.

Rockets (endoreactors):
    engineType idealRocket;          (example: etc/inputs/input_idealRocket)
  - ambientProperties { pressure; }  ambient pressure in Pa, 0 = vacuum
  - hotGasProperties { gamma; R; }   combustion products, required (cp = gamma R/(gamma-1) if omitted)
  - chamber { pressure; temperature; mixture_ratio; }  p_c [Pa], T_c [K], O/F (optional)
    plus exactly one of: thrust [N], mass_flow [kg/s], throat_diameter [m]
  - nozzle { area_ratio; efficiency; }  Ae/At of the convergent-divergent nozzle;
    without area_ratio the nozzle is adapted to ambient pressure (not allowed in vacuum)
  Output: c*, throat and exit states, C_F, Isp (ambient and vacuum), mass flows, throat/exit diameters.
  Over-expanded nozzles are checked for flow separation (Summerfield) and shocks inside the nozzle.
  In the GUI pick "Rocket" in the top bar: thrust-chamber drawing to scale, T-s, p/T/Mach along the
  nozzle (isentropic quasi-1D on a representative contour) and sweeps of Isp, C_F and exit Mach.

Solid rocket motors:
    engineType solidRocket;          (example: etc/inputs/input_solidRocket)
  - propellantProperties { density; flame_temperature; burn_rate_coefficient; burn_rate_exponent; }
    rho_p [kg/m3], T_c [K], burn rate r [mm/s] = a * p[MPa]^n
  - hotGasProperties { gamma; R; }   as for idealRocket
  - grain { segments; outer_diameter; core_diameter; segment_length; inhibited_ends; }  BATES grain [m],
    outer surface bonded to the case; the end faces burn unless inhibited_ends True
  - nozzle { throat_diameter; area_ratio; efficiency; }  the area ratio is required
  Model (quasi-steady, no ignition transient, no tail-off, gas mass in the chamber neglected):
    d = d0 + 2x,  L = L0 - 2x (L = L0 with inhibited ends)
    Ab = N [pi d L + 2 pi/4 (D^2 - d^2)]          (end term only with burning ends)
    web = min((D - d0)/2, L0/2)                   ((D - d0)/2 with inhibited ends)
    c* = sqrt(R Tc / gamma) ((gamma+1)/2)^((gamma+1)/(2(gamma-1)))
    Kn = Ab/At,  pc = (a rho_p c* Kn)^(1/(1-n))   (a in SI: a[mm/s] 1e-3 / (1e6)^n)
    r = a pc^n,  dt = dx / r,  F = C_F pc At with the nozzle relations of idealRocket
    I_tot = integral of F dt,  Isp = I_tot / (m_p g0),  m_p = rho_p N pi/4 (D^2 - d0^2) L0
  The console prints Ab, At, Kn, pc, r and F at ignition for hand checks; the GUI stations,
  T-s and nozzle profile refer to the maximum-pressure point.

Cigarette (end-burning) grains with layers of different propellants:
    engineType endBurner;            (example: etc/inputs/input_endBurner, boost + sustain)
  - hotGasProperties { gamma; R; }   common combustion products
  - grain { diameter; layer1 { ... } layer2 { ... } ... }  layer1 faces the nozzle and burns first
    each layer: length; density; flame_temperature; burn_rate_coefficient; burn_rate_exponent;
    optional gamma; R; cp (default: the common ones)
  - nozzle { throat_diameter; area_ratio; efficiency; }
  Quasi-steady phases, one per layer, with an instantaneous change of pressure between them:
    Ab = pi D^2/4,  Kn = Ab/At,  pc_i = (a_i rho_i c*_i Kn)^(1/(1-n_i)),  r_i = a_i pc_i^n_i,
    t_i = L_i / r_i,  F_i = C_F,i pc_i At,  I_tot = sum F_i t_i
  The console and the GUI ("Burn history" table) list every phase for hand checks.

Graphical interface:
    python3 projetGUI.py            (opens http://127.0.0.1:8050 in the browser)
    python3 projetGUI.py --port 9000 --no-browser
  Requires flask and plotly (pip install flask plotly); works offline.
  - edit inputs per component, results update live
  - engine schematic coloured by temperature, T-s diagram, station table (CSV export)
  - p/T profiles, energy balance (Sankey), parametric sweeps, baseline comparison
  - exam mode (hides results) and exam text generation
  - import/export of the OpenFOAM-like input files
  - inputs and results are checked (gui/validation.py): errors block the run,
    warnings flag values outside the usual range; the CLI prints the same messages

Tests:
    python3 -m pytest
  tests/test_projet.py re-derives every component equation from the solver output,
  checks the ideal Brayton cycle, first/second law, mode round-trips, scaling,
  hundreds of random engines, the input validation and the GUI API.
